<p id="S6.T3.5" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><em id="S6.T3.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Notes.</em> RS entries (SmoothAdv/CAF) are <em id="S6.T3.5.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">certified</em> accuracies; DRIFT entries are <em id="S6.T3.5.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">empirical</em> accuracies under <math id="S6.T3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> PGD-EOT attacks at the same
radii. Certified and empirical numbers should not be compared as if equivalent guarantees.</p></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4 </span>Ablation Studies</h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To better understand the contribution of each component in DRIFT, we conduct ablation experiments by selectively including or excluding loss terms during training. Table <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 6.4 Ablation Studies ‣ 6 Results and Analysis ‣ DRIFT: Divergent Response in Filtered Transformations for Robust Adversarial Defense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> reports robust accuracy under both non-adaptive and adaptive PGD attacks for four different models. We begin with a baseline that combines standard cross-entropy loss <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{CE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{CE}</annotation></semantics></math> with adversarial training <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{adv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{adv}</annotation></semantics></math>. While this setup provides reasonable non-adaptive robustness (e.g., <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75.66\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>75.66</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75.66\%</annotation></semantics></math> on ResNet-v2), it collapses almost completely under adaptive attacks (below <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math> across all models). Introducing Jacobian-Space Separation (<math id="S6.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{JS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{JS}</annotation></semantics></math>) substantially improves adaptive robustness, boosting performance to <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="39.80\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>39.80</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">39.80\%</annotation></semantics></math> on ResNet-v2 and similar gains across other architectures. Logit-VJP Separation (<math id="S6.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{LVJP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{LVJP}</annotation></semantics></math>) proves even more effective, further elevating adaptive robustness to <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="47.61\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>47.61</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">47.61\%</annotation></semantics></math> on ResNet-v2 and consistently outperforming <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{JS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{JS}</annotation></semantics></math> across models. Finally, combining all three components (<math id="S6.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{CE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{CE}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{JS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{JS}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{LVJP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{LVJP}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{adv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{adv}</annotation></semantics></math>) yields the strongest defense. This full configuration achieves the highest adaptive robustness across all architectures, with ResNet-v2 at <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="53.78\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>53.78</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">53.78\%</annotation></semantics></math>, Inception-v3 at <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50.40\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50.40</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50.40\%</annotation></semantics></math>, DeiT-S at <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="48.15\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>48.15</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">48.15\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and ViT-B/16 at <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="56.74\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>56.74</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">56.74\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Importantly, this robustness comes at no cost to non-adaptive performance, which remains on par with or slightly better than the baselines.</p>
</div>
