<div id="A1.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Non-adaptive results provide useful signal about baseline robustness but can overestimate true security; adaptive, defense-aware attacks (e.g., BPDA+EOT) are reported elsewhere in this paper for completeness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.3.2 </span>Impact of EOT</h4>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify the effect of expectation over transformation (EOT) on a stochastic defense, we run PGD-<math id="A1.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\!\in\!\{5,10,20\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\!\in\!\{5,10,20\}</annotation></semantics></math> EOT samples per gradient step (common randomness across paired evaluations; all other hyperparameters fixed). As <math id="A1.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the attack better estimates the gradient of the <em id="A1.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">expected</em> loss and therefore becomes stronger. Table <a href="#A1.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ A.3.2 Impact of EOT ‣ A.3 More Analysis ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ DRIFT: Divergent Response in Filtered Transformations for Robust Adversarial Defense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> reports Top-1 robust accuracy (%) across <math id="A1.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span><span id="A1.T9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PGD-EOT on ViT-B/16 (ImageNet).</span> Increasing EOT samples strengthens the attack, lowering robust accuracy. <math id="A1.T9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> columns show absolute point drops.</figcaption>
